//RS /// D-SERDE2=A / I9: lower the compiler-written codecs an IMPORTED module declares.
//RS ///
//RS /// A generated codec is a top-level `Item::Impl` carrying the parser-unforgeable
//RS /// `is_generated_serde` provenance — the same authority the entry-module
//RS /// `Item::Impl` arm below and `Codegen/Items.rs::emit_trait_method` already treat
//RS /// as the coverage decision for compiler-written code. The imported-module loop
//RS /// matches only `Item::Func`, `Item::CodeModule`, `Item::Struct`/`Item::Enum` and
//RS /// the unit-label `Display` impl, so without this pass AOT emits an imported
//RS /// type's `Encode`/`Decode` while the JIT program and the interpreter have no
//RS /// method at all: encoding a struct with an imported field succeeds under
//RS /// `jet build` and stops at E0956 under the default `jet run`. One program, two
//RS /// meanings by tier — the I9 split this closes.
//RS ///
//RS /// The codec is lowered against its DECLARING context. That context owns
//RS /// private nominals that an importing context must not expose, while
//RS /// `register_own_struct_shapes` also registers each local leaf under its
//RS /// canonical bundle identity. The generated body, its return type, and every
//RS /// imported call therefore use one nominal identity without making private
//RS /// declarations visible to consumers.
